xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Login: the tolerant-load shadowed-class neutralisation (#14387) now
    also covers `system services web-management api-auth` users, named
    keys, and the api-auth default class (#16074). A custom class
    shadowing a built-in (e.g. `class super-user { permissions view; }`)
    is inert at runtime, so an api-auth credential naming the shadowed
    class kept the built-in's permissions on tolerant load / peer sync;
    it is now neutralised to `unauthorized` with a warning, exactly like
    `system login user` assignments. Strict commit still rejects the
    shadowing definition.

  * Login: argument-scoped allow/deny-commands regexes now fire on the
    gRPC and REST surfaces (#16067). Both gates rebuild the full
    canonicalised command from the decoded request (ShowText topic
    parameters, Ping/Traceroute targets, session and match-policies
    selectors, ShowText filters) and judge it with the argument-free
    charge, so `^show route table secret-vrf`, `^ping 10\.99\.` and
    `^show log .*` deny remotely exactly as on the box; a request whose
    arguments cannot be reconstructed denies for a restricted class.
    REST show-text topics now resolve through the same longest-prefix
    rule as gRPC. The commit-time unenforceability advisory and the
    once-per-class runtime warning now consider argument-bearing
    spellings via per-surface registered argument samples, so an
    argument-scoped restriction that fires remotely is no longer
    reported as on-box-only.
  * NAT: pre-routing NAT no longer translates under the fabric
    fallback identity for ambiguous (unpromoted) fabric arrivals
    (#16045). Static/pool DNAT, persistent-NAT, NPTv6 and NAT64 were
    judged under the local fabric/logical zone while the later policy
    promoted the claimed zone, so a fabric-scoped rule fired for a
    zone pair that never selected it. Scoped matchers now fail closed
    under ambiguous provenance (pool DNAT poison-drop, static/NPTv6/
    NAT64 withheld, persistent reader skipped); unscoped rules and
    native owner-scope ingress are unchanged, and the claimed zone is
    never granted before owner-RG proof.
  * Drop IPv6 packets in the AF_XDP shim when their extension-header chain
    exceeds the walk limit, instead of passing an unresolved protocol with
    zero ports to the kernel for local, multicast, or link-local
    destinations. Resolvable chains are unaffected (#16038).
  * PPTP ALG: the GRE data-channel resolve now looks associations up
    in the routing domain of the claimed fabric identity the flow is
    re-keyed to (validated or ambiguous zone), not the fabric link's
    own ingress domain (#16055). On an ambiguous fabric arrival the
    same call ID in another tenant's domain is no longer claimed or
    idle-refreshed, and a fabric arrival carrying no claimed zone
    resolves in domain 0 (fail closed) rather than the fabric link's
    domain.
  * Cap the cluster bulk-sync receive window at the forward-session table
    capacity and expire a window held open past an hour. The cap is on
    live occupancy (a delete inside the window frees its slot), the
    deadline is enforced on receive, on the receive-loop read-timeout
    tick (so a silent peer still expires), and at BulkEnd, and an
    aborted window latches bulk-incomplete readiness debt until a later
    bulk reconciles instead of clearing the failover blocker. Distinct
    keys past the cap are counted and dropped without storing; the
    transient-failure marker sets share the cap. New counters
    BulkRecvCapped and BulkRecvAborted render in cluster status when
    nonzero (#16039).
  * PPTP ALG: control-segment teaching now requires an in-window TCP
    data sequence, judged against the pre-note tracked state
    (#16037). An admitted but out-of-window Outgoing-Call-Reply no
    longer installs attacker-chosen call IDs, and a synced session
    with no sequence state learns nothing. `no-sequence-check`
    opts out, as for closing segments.
  * Cluster: session-sync peer liveness now refreshes only on
    semantically accepted frames, and every sync message type carries a
    per-type payload cap (#16041). Well-framed but rejected frames
    (unknown types, truncated records, stale full-sets, impossible
    clock readings) previously reset the missed-heartbeat count and the
    last-receive stamp before dispatch, so a faulty keyed peer could
    mask its own failover trigger indefinitely. Over-cap headers are
    refused before the payload is read, counted as
    OversizeFramesRefused in cluster statistics, and drop the
    connection; unknown types remain globally capped and ignored for
    forward compatibility.
  * Sessions: an in-window but non-exact RST no longer stamps session
    teardown (#16040). The closing-segment sequence verdict now
    requires an exact sequence match when the RST bit is set (RFC 9293
    §3.5.3 / RFC 5961 §3.2), compared against the endpoint's tracked
    receive-next (learned from the peer direction's ACK field, so
    downstream-loss recovery RSTs still close) rather than the
    firewall-observed send-end — closing the ~window-size blind-guess
    teardown left by #15947 leg1. FIN keeps receive-window validity,
    `no-sequence-check` still bypasses the verdict, and entries with
    no tracked sequence/ACK state are still admitted.

  * NAT: freed PAT ports on small source pools (<=256 ports per
    address) now sit out a 60 s minimum reuse delay before any new
    flow can claim them (#16035). Previously the occupancy bit cleared
    and the port recycled FIFO with no quarantine, so on a tiny or
    high-churn pool a delayed UDP reply reusing the old
    (translated address, port, remote) tuple could be delivered to a
    new owner of the same port. Large pools are unchanged (their
    FIFO-after-fresh-range reuse already spaces reissue beyond the
    delay), and no protocol is exempted at the allocator layer.
    Fold: repeat frees refresh the token's stamp to the latest free,
    the keyed probe runs under the recycle mutex so a concurrent free
    cannot race its quarantine snapshot, persistent-clear and
    short-timeout GC frees are stamped like flow frees, specific-port
    reserves honour the delay (a synced flow re-joining its own
    untombstoned lease on the same port is exempt), and collision
    requeues restore the dedup bit so no duplicate token can form.
  * NAT: interface SNAT can no longer mint on a static-NAT external
    (#16034). Strict commit already rejected the pair; the Rust
    dataplane now refuses such interface mints (address-only and PAT,
    including after an attempted PAT) and dynamic HA-synced interface
    imports with InterfaceIdentityExhausted on tolerated installs
    (lenient load / peer-sync), where the collision previously
    published a duplicate wire tuple. A port-mapped static is PAT'd
    around and refused only on its exact port; genuine static imports
    keep their no-reservation success. The NPTv6 leg was #16015.
  * Sessions: handshake promotion/completion now validates the TCP ack
    number (#16042). A SYN-ACK or completing ACK whose ack does not equal
    the companion direction's SYN ack end (ISN + 1 + SYN payload,
    wrapping) no longer promotes a session off the opening window at any
    gate — lookup, flow-cache hit, keepalive, or HA update/promote. Each
    direction's ack end is learned from its SYN/SYN-ACK (Owner-admitted
    notes only, so a rejected SYN-ACK cannot launder evidence) or seeded
    at install (both halves, through the NAT reverse key, so translated
    pairs validate identically). Companion resolution runs only for
    handshake-pending pairs. Unknown evidence (mid-stream pickup, HA
    import, unparsed ack) fails open on the opening window, so a
    failover import's first completing segment still completes.
  * Cluster: an open control-key rotation now ages visibly and alarms
    when forgotten (#16029). UpdateConfig stamps when the current
    additional-authentication-key window opened (keyed on the additional
    key's identity, so re-applying the identical config cannot launder
    the age; swapping the additional key re-anchors, finalize clears).
    `show chassis cluster statistics` renders the window's age on the
    Key rotation line, and past 7 days the line carries an ALARM naming
    the window by key ID — the retired key is still accepted, so a
    forgotten step-5 finalize previously left it valid indefinitely.
    The alarm never auto-finalizes (retiring a key the peer still signs
    with reopens the dual-master window); the stamp is in-memory and
    re-anchors at the first apply after a daemon restart, so a stale
    alarm can be up to one restart late, never early.
  * Logging: unify the raw-event wire parser behind logEvent and
    DecodeRawEventRecord (#16127). parseRawEvent is now the single
    offset parser (zero-allocation, caller-owned rawEvent) and
    formatRawEventAddrs the single address renderer, so the live and
    decode-only paths cannot diverge on offsets or family rendering.
    Unknown-address-family policy is explicit and pinned: only
    AF_INET (2) / AF_INET6 (10) are accepted — the decoder rejects and
    the live path drops any other family byte, replacing the former
    live-only "non-v6 means IPv4" fallback. Golden short / legacy-144 /
    extended / IPv6 frames pin live-vs-decoded parity. Fanout is
    unchanged (parser-only unification).
  * Fabric: GetSessions is no longer admitted tokenless during the
    rollout grace (#16033). A keyed node whose peer has never
    authenticated previously grace-admitted GetSessions for up to five
    minutes per anchor event, exposing the full session table (complete
    5-tuples, NAT bindings and counters) to any on-segment host with no
    token in peer-down, replacement and rollout windows. GetSessions now
    requires a valid fabric token at all times; GetStatus,
    GetSessionSummary, GetZonePairSummary, MonitorInterface and the
    fabric-safe ShowText topic (counts/statistics only) remain
    grace-eligible, and a keyed peer's valid-token GetSessions proxy is
    unchanged.
  * Fabric: legacy method-only MonitorInterface streams are now rejected
    and open fabric streams re-authenticate continuously (#16031).
    Fabric streams must carry the args-bound token matching the request
    (missing/mismatched is Unauthenticated); the counted fallback and
    xpf_fabric_stream_args_unbound_total are removed. Local loopback
    monitor streams are unaffected. Open fabric streams re-verify every
    5s that the stream token's minting key is still in the accepted-key
    set (checked at the token's captured mint window, so window expiry
    alone never kills a healthy stream): removing the cluster PSK ends
    a stream within one tick, while a rotation still accepting the old
    key does not.
  * userspace-dp: GRE/WG shared outer-fragment pool can no longer be
    starved by tail fragments or one peer source (#16028). Pool state
    creation is now head-only: a GRE head creates an entry only after
    the full tunnel-endpoint matcher (GRE key, transport domain,
    resolved transport egress, ingress host-inbound/zone admission)
    passes, a WG head only after its listener/zone/ownership gates,
    and tails consult the pool without ever creating state. One outer
    source may hold at most half the 64-entry shared pool (no eviction
    of other sources), so a spoofed-peer tail/head flood cannot starve
    WireGuard or other GRE peers. Trade-off: a tail arriving before
    its head is now dropped instead of buffered (pre-head tail
    buffering was the starvation vector); correctly ordered fragmented
    tunnel traffic is unaffected. Fold: head-admission Key reads are
    bounded by the declared outer length (never trailing capture);
    probe counter charges reuse a per-thread scratch store (no
    per-head allocation); consult-only tail drops are counted in the
    pool drop counter. Accepted restriction, tested: a KEYED GRE
    header split across the first fragments (Key bytes beyond the
    head fragment) is refused at the head — the Key cannot be
    verified there; unkeyed (K-clear) split headers reassemble
    normally. Fold 2: a K-present split head is refused even against
    an unkeyed endpoint — the matcher can never accept K-present for
    a key-0 endpoint, so admitting it held guaranteed-dead state.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Cluster: heartbeat anti-replay latch now survives a daemon restart
    (#16022). The peer epoch-capable latch, boot-epoch floor and its
    bound sessions are persisted in a generation-ordered, key-set-scoped
    record alongside ha-boot-epoch. Admission queues one coalescing
    asynchronous persistence worker; heartbeat and shutdown paths never
    perform or join filesystem I/O, and guarded writes skip if a newer
    generation is already on disk. Pre-#16022 a receiver restart cleared
    the latch/floor, and 65 captured sender
    incarnations replayed while the peer was down churned the 64-entry
    ring indefinitely (1625/1625 epochless admitted); the restored latch
    refuses the epochless set and the restored floor+bindings bound the
    epoch-bearing one to one finite ascending pass. The record is scoped
    to the accepted-key-set digest, so rotating the control-link PSK
    clears it on the next apply — the rollback recovery is now the
    rotation alone, with no state file to hand-edit; a failed or wedged
    write fails open to the previous process-scoped behaviour. Rollback
    recovery text, NoteEpochDowngradeHeartbeat and README residuals
    updated accordingly.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Sessions: pressure shedding is protocol-symmetric and keyed on
    never-replied state (#16019). Rotating spoofed-source UDP/ICMP
    singles previously filled the session table while only a TCP
    initial-SYN newcomer could shed handshake-incomplete victims;
    now any qualifying new flow (TCP initial SYN, or any non-TCP first
    packet) at the 90% watermark sheds unreplied local sessions of
    any protocol, and replied/established flows are never victims.
    Fold: victim reply evidence is node-level (shared-map reverse
    row rev counter > 0 + peer-synced fence), not just the shedding
    worker's counters or reverse-row existence; retained victims have
    their queued Close delta cancelled and deferred sheds recheck the
    reply predicate; the stale-candidate scan per admission is bounded.
    Fold 3: the reverse shared row itself is stamped with the forward
    entry's rev counter at publish/promote/reverse-install, so the
    cross-worker proof is readable on the row the check consults.
  * NAT: persistent-NAT permit tightening now revokes stored
    any-remote grants on allocator reuse (#16017). A reused pool
    allocator stamps its any-remote buckets with a permit version;
    tightening to target-host/target-host-port on rebuild leaves the
    stamp stale so inbound lookup denies non-target remotes, while
    an unchanged any-remote permit restamps and preserves the grant.
    Exact target-host/port grants remain permitted under tighter modes.
  * NAT: pool/interface SNAT can no longer mint inside an NPTv6
    external prefix (#16015). Strict commit rejects a referenced
    source-pool / NAT64-pool / interface-egress IPv6 member inside an
    installed NPTv6 external prefix (lenient load warns); the Rust
    dataplane refuses such pool, interface, and HA-synced mints with
    PoolPeerAddressOverlap. NPTv6 publishes the prefix statelessly
    with the port unchanged, so the minted tuple previously collided
    with the NPTv6-published tuple for a different host.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium
  * Policy: a NoRoute flow (no userspace FIB egress) now adjudicates
    the known ingress's exact-pair deny/reject rules (deny-only;
    exact-pair permits stay delegated) and the egress-unconstrained
    tiers — `to-zone any`, both-any, and `junos-global` with an
    `Any` to-zone scope — before the default policy decides, so under
    `default-policy permit-all` an explicit matching deny/reject
    (including `trust->untrust deny`) drops the packet instead of
    delegating it to the kernel FIB (#16021). `from-zone any
    to-zone <z>` and to-zone-scoped globals stay ineligible without
    an egress zone, and an unmatched NoRoute flow still follows the
    default policy.

  * Fold (#16350): production PAT allocation now skips peer-owned
    candidates during the keyed probe (bounded by addresses x range,
    fail-closed with PoolPeerAddressOverlap on exclusion-exhaustion)
    instead of mint/rollback retrying the same port; NAT64 gained the
    same exclusion entry point, and the keyed helpers compile in
    production builds (not only under cfg(test)).


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NAT PAT port allocation is no longer predictable (#16011,
    RFC 6056): pool PAT claims, the interface-mode PAT walk and the
    stateless try-next-port path now start from a keyed hash of the
    flow tuple under a per-boot secret drawn from the OS CSPRNG, with
    a bounded fail-closed probe; the monotonic sequential cursor is
    removed. Deterministic CGNAT block allocation keeps its
    documented lowest-free-in-block contract, and freed-port recycle
    remains FIFO-queued.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Screen: tolerant bad numerics no longer zero syn-flood subfields or
    limit-session (#16006, A3-M3). A bad syn-flood
    alarm/source/destination-threshold or timeout now keeps its Junos
    default (512/4000/4000/20) on the tolerant load/peer-sync path
    instead of falling to disabled behind a warning; a bad limit-session
    value has no default, so the profile is treated as unresolved and
    referencing zones get the substituted conservative default
    (malformed-packet checks only) rather than the profile with a cap
    silently off — preserving any authored alarm-without-drop modifier
    through that unresolved path so a substituted drop is logged and
    forwarded, not enforced. Strict commit still rejects every bad
    value.
  * NAT: persistent leases are now charged per source (#16013). The
    pool allocator refuses a NEW persistent lease once one
    (src_ip, routing_scope) holds 4096 leases (live or idle-retained),
    on the local PAT and address-only paths, HA-synced reserves,
    idle-lease imports, and deterministic NAT64 BIBs; joining an
    existing lease is unaffected. Previously only the pool-global cap
    applied, so one host's open/close churn could retain the whole
    lease table (and a single-address pool's ports) for the 300s-24h
    idle timeout while other sources were refused.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Config: a literal `apply-groups-except` naming no defined group no
    longer silently no-ops (#16003). The exclusion still removes nothing
    and never errors (a shared cluster config may exclude a group only
    the peer defines), but compilation now warns once per distinct
    unresolved literal name on strict, tolerant, and per-node paths.
    Dynamic `${...}` exclusions stay silent, since another node's view
    may define the group they resolve to there.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Screen: correct stale pre-#7168 fail-open comments at the dangling
    screen-profile gate sites (#16005, A3-M2). Since #7168 the dataplane
    substitutes ScreenProfile::conservative_default() (nine threshold-free
    malformed-packet checks) for an unresolved profile; the residual is
    loss of flood/scan/session-limit thresholds on the tolerant leg, not
    loss of all screening. Strict still rejects; the tolerant warning now
    is pinned by test to name that threshold loss, and the gate-site
    comments/docs no longer assert ScreenVerdict::Pass fail-open.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * DHCP: honour `system services dhcp-local-server` /
    `dhcpv6-local-server group <g> interface <if> exclude` (#16004).
    The leaf was parsed but silently ignored, so the excluded
    interface was still handed to Kea in interfaces-config and
    served. The compiler now omits an interface carrying `exclude`
    (flat-set, hierarchical, braced-body, bare-block, and bracket
    spellings, both families); other per-interface modifiers remain
    parsed-only and are never promoted to interface names.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Snapshot summary counts verified (#16056, A6-06): the Rust
    apply handler now refuses a snapshot whose summary
    interface_count/zone_count disagree with the built sections,
    and refuses an empty interfaces/zones install over a non-empty
    installed set (both-zeroed shape), keeping prior forwarding
    and counting the refusal on snapshot_policy_mismatch_total.
    Go re-derives InterfaceCount/ZoneCount from the built sections
    (post-quarantine for zones) in the full builder, the link
    refresh, and the apply-time rebuild, and the send gate refuses
    a mismatch known-unsent. Protocol v67 -> v68 fences a helper
    that would ignore the counts and install a dropped section.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Firewall: poison the uncovered-family `except` arm on tolerant load
    (#16001, EX-4). A sole-`except` prefix-list covering no address of
    its pool's family (v4-only `except` under `family any` / `inet6`)
    matched every packet of the uncovered family; the term is now
    marked per-pool and the userspace snapshot plus both lo0 mirrors
    refuse the candidate whole, retaining prior-good state. Positive
    single-family lists still warn-but-apply.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Harness: a refused or failed cluster deploy no longer leaves later
    gates with a bare exe_check=UNAVAILABLE VOID (#16290). cmd_deploy
    arms an EXIT-trap guard after manifest invalidation that records a
    .failed sidecar naming the deploy target and failed step; the gate
    surfaces it in the VOID reason. The manifest itself stays absent
    (fail-closed, no stale provenance revived); a successful deploy
    clears the marker.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Docs/harness: record the #16217 aged-failover root-cause chain
    (seed/FIB/supersession/split-handshake/wire-stage/neighbor legs
    closed, receiving-worker HA-inactive leg still open) in
    docs/ha-failover-status.md and add the never-again aged cell
    (--aged-failover: 30s stream age, zero zero-intervals) to
    scripts/userspace-ha-failover-validation.sh. PR #16217 remains
    unmerged and is not an aged-failover cure.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Meter LocalDelivery input policers before the miss-path PERMIT
    FilterLog, lo0, and junos-host side effects (#15949, H34-04
    arm 2): a red host-bound packet now drops at the early gate with
    a truthful DENY log, no lo0/junos-host counter increments, no
    premature closing-pair eviction, and persistent-hold rollback.
    Conforming packets meter exactly once; their handles are stored
    on the installed session for hit replay.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * DHCP relay: add bounded per-sender fairness ahead of the
    per-interface aggregate token bucket (#16087, folded 6x on
    #16322). One sender sustaining the maximum-packet-rate no longer
    starves other clients: each chaddr / Client-ID DUID (both peeked
    from the wire, pre-parse) gets a 1/256 share of the rate (floored
    at 1 pps, burst at least 8) in a 256-entry table, with a banked-
    refill waiter rotation so a competing sender progresses even at
    1 pps. No aggregate refund: the aggregate stays the hard
    pre-parse bound and fairness is additional to it. Stops a naive
    single-identity flood; identity rotation still needs switch
    controls (pkg/dhcprelay/README.md).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Convert every exhaustive StaticNATRuleSnapshot (35) and
    ThreeColorPolicerSnapshot (5) literal to a
    `..Default::default()` tail, dropping only default-valued
    fields, and tighten both additive-wire ceilings to 0
    (#16267); also tighten the already-zero NAT64RuleSnapshot
    ceiling 3 -> 0 and lower the population non-vacuity floor
    80 -> 25 for the literals removed/miscounted. Test/fixture
    literals only; no wire or runtime behaviour changes.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Verify SO_PEERCRED on the Kea lease-sync control-socket dial
    (#16058): the client previously bare-dialed the socket pathname,
    so a wrong/squatted server was trusted as Kea and its fabricated
    leases fed HA lease sync. The dial now refuses any peer that is
    not the resolved Kea runtime user (_kea/kea), root, or the
    daemon itself, before any command byte is written.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add DDNS require-tsig opt-in and a live unsigned-posture gauge
    (#15992): strict commit refuses require-tsig with a non-loopback
    RFC2136 update-server and no tsig-key (both DHCP and Surface A
    surfaces; DHCP including a disabled family, which still withdraws
    persisted records), the backend refuses the UPDATE before
    sending on the tolerant path, and
    xpf_dhcp_ddns_unsigned_nonloopback_active /
    xpf_ddns_surface_a_unsigned_nonloopback_active stay at 1 while
    the forgeable-rcode posture persists, including owned records
    pending unsigned cleanup after disable/removal and seeded at
    restart from persisted ownership. Default unsigned
    source-IP-ACL publishing is unchanged.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Load an `ascii-text` IPsec PSK that starts with `0x`/`0s` as its
    literal bytes (#15991): the gateway-group secret fold now carries
    each member PSK's authored encoding (pskValue{value, hex}) and
    emits a loader-facing token, because swanctl strips the quotes
    then hex/base64-decodes any `0x…`/`0s…` value — so a colliding
    ASCII value (e.g. `0x61626364`) renders base64-armored as
    `secret = "0sMHg2MTYyNjM2NA=="`, which charon decodes back to the
    literal string, instead of the raw hex bytes; an ASCII member no
    longer collapses into a hexadecimal member with the same digits.
    Hexadecimal keys (#15027) and all other PSK renders are unchanged.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Document that in-VM tcpdump is blind to AF_XDP fast-path flows
    (#16111): testing-docs/failover-testing.md now names the working
    observation points (LAN/WAN host and gRPC endpoint captures, plus
    helper-statistics, Last resolution / Recent exceptions, monitor
    interface, and session deltas on both nodes), qualifies aggregate
    counters as discriminating per flow only for an isolated/controlled
    workload or when corroborated (busy-node per-flow proof requires
    per-flow counters or session deltas), and forbids reading
    `0 packets captured` as "traffic never arrived"; the failover-test
    and iperf-grpc-tcpdump skills and the historical HA archive carry
    the same warning, capture_iperf.sh labels its firewall-side
    captures as blind in summary.txt, and a docsref guard test fails
    if the guidance is removed. Docs/skills only; no dataplane change.
  * Delete the two dead FRR render wrappers
    generatePolicyOptionsForSection and resolveRedistributeChain
    (#16205): both were definition-only — production already calls
    the Cluster15158/15157 inner forms directly. Update the #9510
    redistribution-AFI canary in the same change to prove the live
    cluster chain resolver's self-forwarding instead of referencing
    the deleted wrapper, so the census stays non-vacuous.
  * Key session and flow-export interface identity by the unit's own
    device (#16065): the shared config.SessionIfaceNames derivation
    resolves each unit through ResolveKernelIfName (VLAN children,
    per-unit tunnels, RETH members) with the wire VID only, replacing
    the parent-ifindex + unit-number keys on the CLI, gRPC and REST
    session surfaces. A device shared by two units (wg0.0/wg0.1 on one
    WireGuard TUN) is refused instead of first-wins named, so
    clear-by-interface can no longer delete a sibling unit's sessions
    and flow-export sampling no longer judges both units by one
    sibling's name (daemon buildIfNames projects the same table).
  * Guard the #15947 reverse-only lease release against double
    release and restrict its NAT reconstruction assert to the
    source-NAT shape.

